# YouTube Live Service Module for Matrix Synapse

Creates YouTube live broadcasts on demand, powering the room Live feature.

## Model: dynamic broadcast, single channel

Unlike `room_streams_service`'s `fixed` provider (a channel configured once,
manually), this module creates a **new broadcast every time** someone starts
a Live — inside a single YouTube channel, the company's own.

On YouTube, channel and broadcast are different concepts: a channel can have
many broadcasts over time. There's no need for a channel per room — the
module always creates a new broadcast on the same channel, tied to the
configured credentials.

This module never reads or writes `room_streams` directly — see "Room
validation and persistence" below.

## Flow

1. An admin clicks "Start broadcast" in a room configured with
   `provider: "youtube"`, in Element.
2. The frontend calls `POST /_synapse/youtube_live_service/start_broadcast`.
3. The module confirms the room is configured for YouTube (via
   `room_streams_service`), then authenticates with the YouTube Live
   Streaming API and makes 3 calls:
   - `liveBroadcasts.insert` — creates the broadcast "event" (the video).
   - `liveStreams.insert` — creates the ingestion "pipe", which generates the
     RTMP server and the stream key.
   - `liveBroadcasts.bind` — links the two together.
4. The response carries the `watch_url` (to build the widget/player) and the
   `ingestion_address` + `stream_key` (for the admin to paste into OBS).
5. The broadcast is persisted back to `room_streams` (again via
   `room_streams_service`), so it's recoverable later even if this response
   never reaches the client.

The module **does not configure OBS** — that's still done manually by the
person streaming, pasting the received key. This is inherent to any
streaming service, not a limitation of this module.

## Room validation and persistence

`start_broadcast` doesn't touch `room_streams` itself — it calls two methods
on `room_streams_service`'s instance (obtained from `api._hs`, see that
module's README for the cross-module pattern and the required load order in
`homeserver.yaml`):

- **`require_youtube_room(room_id)`**, before calling YouTube: `404` if the
  room has no stream configuration, `400` if it's configured with a
  different provider. This runs before the (expensive, side-effecting) call
  to YouTube, so a request against a nonexistent or misconfigured room fails
  cheaply instead of creating an orphan broadcast on the company channel.
- **`set_youtube_broadcast(room_id, broadcast_id, watch_url)`**, after
  YouTube confirms creation. If this write fails, the request still returns
  `200`: the broadcast already exists for real on YouTube at this point, and
  withholding the stream key from the admin would be worse than losing the
  `room_streams` association. The failure is logged at `ERROR`.

## Endpoint

`POST /_synapse/youtube_live_service/start_broadcast`

**Admin-only** — follows the same pattern as the rest of the system (creating
a room/space, enabling features, configuring the fixed channel). A room-local
moderator who isn't a global admin cannot start a broadcast.

**Body:**
```json
{ "room_id": "!room:example.com", "title": "My live" }
```

**Response (200):**
```json
{
    "room_id": "!room:example.com",
    "broadcast_id": "abc123XYZ_9",
    "watch_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=abc123XYZ_9",
    "ingestion_address": "rtmp://a.rtmp.youtube.com/live2",
    "stream_key": "xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"
}
```

**Errors:**
- `400` — missing `room_id` or `title`, or the room is configured with a
  provider other than `youtube`.
- `403` — the caller is not an admin.
- `404` — the room has no stream configuration at all (never called
  `set_stream`).
- `503` — no working YouTube client is configured: credentials are missing
  or unreadable, and `youtube_use_mock` was not set. See "Mock vs real
  client" below.
- `502` — the call to the YouTube API failed (network, expired credential,
  quota exceeded, account not yet cleared for live streaming, etc.).

## Mock vs real client

`build_youtube_client` (in `module.py`) picks one of three implementations,
in this order:

1. **`youtube_use_mock: true`** → `MockYoutubeClient`, which returns fake
   data (same shape as the real API). For local development only. Logs a
   `WARNING` every time it's used, so it's never mistaken for a real
   broadcast. This is opt-in on purpose: earlier versions fell back to the
   mock automatically whenever credentials were missing, so a secret
   mounted at the wrong path in production would silently hand an admin a
   fake, working-looking stream key.
2. **`youtube_client_id`, `youtube_client_secret_file` and
   `youtube_refresh_token_file` all present and readable** → `RealYoutubeClient`,
   which calls the real API. The full 3-step call chain (create broadcast,
   create stream, bind) has been verified against the real YouTube API,
   returning a real broadcast, ingestion address and stream key. Going live
   end-to-end (encoder → YouTube → viewer) has not been verified: the test
   account used was blocked by YouTube's own `livePermissionBlocked`
   anti-spam restriction (see step 7 below) until the broadcast-creation
   step above was confirmed working.
3. **Anything else** (missing or unreadable config, `youtube_use_mock` not
   set) → `UnconfiguredYoutubeClient`. Logs an `ERROR` naming which config
   keys are missing, and `start_broadcast` returns `503`.

## Configuration

```yaml
modules:
  # Must come first: this module looks up room_streams_service's instance
  # via api._hs at load time.
  - module: modules.room_streams_service.module.RoomStreamsServiceModule
    config:
      admin_user_id: "@admin:localhost"
      admin_token_file: "/data/admin_token.txt"
      homeserver: "http://localhost:3000"

  - module: modules.youtube_live_service.module.YoutubeLiveServiceModule
    config:
      # client_id is not secret (it's public by nature in the OAuth flow),
      # so it can live directly in the yaml.
      youtube_client_id: "123456789.apps.googleusercontent.com"
      # client_secret and refresh_token are sensitive: kept in files outside
      # git, following the same pattern as admin_token_file in other modules.
      youtube_client_secret_file: "/path/to/youtube_client_secret.txt"
      youtube_refresh_token_file: "/path/to/youtube_refresh_token.txt"
      # Local development only, without real credentials (returns FAKE
      # broadcasts; never enable in production):
      # youtube_use_mock: true
```

If `room_streams_service` isn't loaded first, `YoutubeLiveServiceModule`
raises `RuntimeError` at startup.

If any of the three credential keys is missing or unreadable, and
`youtube_use_mock` is not set, the module still loads (it doesn't prevent
Synapse from starting) but `start_broadcast` returns `503` until the config
is fixed.

### Obtaining the credentials (prerequisite, outside the code)

1. A company YouTube channel needs to exist.
2. Create a project in Google Cloud Console and enable the **YouTube Data API
   v3** (which includes the Live Streaming API).
3. Generate OAuth 2.0 credentials of type **"Web application"** (not
   "Desktop") — the desktop type doesn't expose an editable redirect URI,
   which is needed for the next step. Add
   `https://developers.google.com/oauthplayground` as an authorized redirect
   URI. This gives you the `client_id` and `client_secret`.
4. If the OAuth consent screen is in "Testing" mode, add the Google account
   that will authorize (the one tied to the company's YouTube channel) as a
   **test user** — otherwise authorization fails with `access_denied`.
5. Authorize those credentials **once** via the
   [OAuth 2.0 Playground](https://developers.google.com/oauthplayground)
   (use your own credentials, scope `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/youtube`,
   then exchange the authorization code for tokens) to obtain a
   `refresh_token`. That token lets the module refresh access automatically
   afterwards, without needing interactive login again.
6. Store `client_secret` and `refresh_token` in plain text files, outside
   git (add them to `.gitignore`, as already done with `admin_token.txt`).
7. **New/unverified accounts may be blocked from live streaming for up to
   24h** (YouTube's own anti-spam restriction, reason
   `livePermissionBlocked`), independent of anything in this module. Using
   an account with prior live-streaming history avoids this wait.
8. **In "Testing" mode, the refresh_token expires after 7 days.** Publish
   the app (OAuth consent screen → "Publish App") before relying on this in
   anything beyond a one-off manual test — otherwise the integration will
   silently stop working a week in. If a refresh later fails with
   `invalid_grant` or `unauthorized_client`, double-check the client_id /
   client_secret pasted into the Playground match `homeserver.yaml` exactly
   — a refresh_token is only valid for the exact credential pair that
   requested it.

## Technical detail: threads

The `google-api-python-client` library is synchronous (blocking), so the
call is dispatched to a thread. Two things to know about how:

- **Which thread pool.** `RealYoutubeClient` takes the module's `api`
  (`ModuleApi`) and calls `await self.api.defer_to_thread(...)`, Synapse's
  own helper, instead of a raw `asyncio.get_event_loop().run_in_executor(...)`
  (not reliable here — Synapse runs on Twisted's reactor, not a plain
  asyncio loop, and this raises `RuntimeError: await wasn't used with
  future`) or `twisted.internet.threads.deferToThread` directly (works, but
  loses the request's logging context, which shows up as `Expected logging
  context ...` warnings and misattributed logs under concurrency).
- **One Google client per call.** `_build_client()` constructs a fresh
  `googleapiclient` Resource (and its underlying `httplib2.Http`) on every
  call instead of caching one on the instance. Neither is documented as
  thread-safe, and two admins starting a broadcast at the same moment could
  otherwise share a connection and get interleaved socket reads. The extra
  cost (one more OAuth token refresh per call) is negligible since starting
  a broadcast is rare.

## Technical detail: scheduledStartTime

The YouTube API requires `snippet.scheduledStartTime` on `liveBroadcasts.insert`
even when the broadcast is meant to start immediately — it does not accept an
implicit "now". The module sends the current UTC time
(`datetime.now(timezone.utc)`, ISO 8601, e.g. `2026-09-22T16:05:29Z`).
Combined with `enableAutoStart: true` in `contentDetails`, the broadcast goes
live automatically as soon as the encoder starts pushing to the ingestion
address — no manual step needed in YouTube Studio.

## Dependencies

```bash
pip install google-auth google-api-python-client
```

Declared in `pyproject.toml` (verified: a clean virtualenv install pulls
both in, required by `matrix-synapse`). Only required when `RealYoutubeClient`
is actually used — `MockYoutubeClient` doesn't depend on them (the Google
imports live inside the method that builds the client, not at the top of
the file).

## Tests

`PYTHONPATH=. pytest modules/youtube_live_service/tests/` — 30 tests:

- `test_youtube_live_service.py`: admin check, response shape with the mock,
  validation of required `room_id`/`title`, the `503` returned when no
  client is configured, and room validation/persistence (`404`/`400` from
  `require_youtube_room` propagate and are checked *before* calling YouTube;
  a successful broadcast is persisted via `set_youtube_broadcast`; a failure
  to persist doesn't fail the request).
- `test_youtube_client.py`: `MockYoutubeClient`'s fake data;
  `_create_broadcast_sync` against a mocked `googleapiclient` Resource
  (request bodies sent, including `scheduledStartTime`, the bind call, error
  propagation); `create_broadcast` (the async wrapper) with an injected
  `api.defer_to_thread`, including that each call builds its own client.
- `test_module_config.py`: `build_youtube_client`'s selection logic (mock
  only when explicitly requested, real client with full credentials,
  `UnconfiguredYoutubeClient` otherwise — including the "secret mounted at
  the wrong path" scenario), and `_read_secret_file` not crashing Synapse
  startup on an unreadable file.

See `room_streams_service`'s README for its own tests covering
`require_youtube_room` and `set_youtube_broadcast` themselves.

## Open questions / pending decisions

- **Simultaneous broadcast limit.** Not yet validated whether the YouTube
  account in use supports multiple simultaneous broadcasts (in case more
  than one room starts a Live at the same time). Google's docs mention up to
  3 broadcasts bound to a single stream; anecdotal reports suggest a
  higher per-channel cap, but this isn't confirmed officially.
- **Broadcast `privacyStatus`.** Currently hardcoded as `"unlisted"` in the
  code (`_create_broadcast_sync`). To be evaluated whether this should be
  configurable or if `unlisted` is final. Note that `unlisted` broadcasts are
  still reachable by anyone with the `watch_url`, directly on YouTube,
  outside of Element — relevant if a Live ever needs to be paywalled.